<p><b>May 8</b>
</p><p>The <b><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Treaty_of_Lond=
on" title=3D"First Treaty of London">First Treaty of London</a></b> was for=
mally agreed on 8 May 1358 at <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind=
sor_Castle" title=3D"Windsor Castle">Windsor Castle</a> in England. Its ter=
ms ended the then 21-year-old <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King=
dom_of_England" title=3D"Kingdom of England">Anglo</a>-<a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_France" title=3D"Kingdom of France">French<=
/a> conflict now known as the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hund=
red_Years%27_War" title=3D"Hundred Years' War">Hundred Years' War</a>. It w=
as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seal_(emblem)" title=3D"Seal (e=
mblem)">sealed</a> by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_III" =
title=3D"Edward III">Edward&#160;III, king of England</a>, and <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_II_of_France" title=3D"John II of France"=
>John&#160;II, king of France</a>; the latter was a prisoner, having been c=
aptured at the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Poitiers"=
 title=3D"Battle of Poitiers">Battle of Poitiers</a> in 1356. The treaty se=
t <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ransom_of_John_II_of_France" tit=
le=3D"Ransom of John II of France">John's ransom</a> at four million <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89cu" title=3D"=C3=89cu">=C3=A9cus</=
a>, the equivalent of the peacetime income of the English Crown for about 2=
0 years. In addition, France was to transfer to England approximately a qua=
rter of its territory, while Edward was to give up his claim to the French =
throne. The first instalment of the ransom was due to be paid on 1 November=
, but with the French government collapsing into anarchy it proved impossib=
le to raise. Edward refused to accept less than full payment and so the tre=
aty lapsed. Subsequent negotiations led to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Second_Treaty_of_London" title=3D"Second Treaty of London">Sec=
ond Treaty of London</a> but its terms were so harsh that the French govern=
ment repudiated it. Hostilities resumed in October 1359, when Edward again =
invaded France. <i>(<b><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Treat=
y_of_London" title=3D"First Treaty of London">Full&#160;article...</a></b>)=
</i>
</p>
