<p><b>May 18</b>
</p>
<div id=3D"mp-tfa-img" style=3D"float: left; margin: 0.5em 0.9em 0.4em 0em;=
">
<div class=3D"thumbinner mp-thumb" style=3D"background: transparent; color:=
 inherit; border: none; padding: 0; max-width: 108px;">
<span typeof=3D"mw:File"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Jupi=
ter_Heliopolitanus_-_Louvre_AO19534_(cropped).jpg" class=3D"mw-file-descrip=
tion" title=3D"Sursock bronze"><img alt=3D"Sursock bronze" src=3D"https://u=
pload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Jupiter_Heliopolitanus_-_L=
ouvre_AO19534_%28cropped%29.jpg/120px-Jupiter_Heliopolitanus_-_Louvre_AO195=
34_%28cropped%29.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"108" height=3D"180" class=
=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"2472" data-file-height=3D"4120"></a=
></span></div></div>
<p>The <b><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sursock_bronze" title=3D=
"Sursock bronze">Sursock bronze</a></b> is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Gilding" title=3D"Gilding">gilded</a> bronze sculptural group of=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jupiter_Optimus_Maximus_Heliopoli=
tanus" title=3D"Jupiter Optimus Maximus Heliopolitanus">Heliopolitan Jupite=
r</a> dating to the 2nd century AD. A miniature of the cult statue that sto=
od in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Jupiter_(Baalb=
ek)" title=3D"Temple of Jupiter (Baalbek)">Great Temple of Baalbek</a>, Leb=
anon, it depicts the god as a beardless youth wearing a <i><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calathus_(basket)" title=3D"Calathus (basket)">kal=
athos</a></i>, a basket-shaped headdress, and an <i><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Ependytes" title=3D"Ependytes">ependytes</a></i>, a close=
-fitting dress, under ornate armor. The front of the armor bears busts of s=
even deities associated with celestial bodies=E2=80=94<a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sol_(Roman_mythology)" title=3D"Sol (Roman mythology)">=
Sol</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna_(goddess)" title=3D"=
Luna (goddess)">Luna</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_(my=
thology)" title=3D"Mars (mythology)">Mars</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Mercury_(mythology)" title=3D"Mercury (mythology)">Mercury</a>=
, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jupiter_(god)" title=3D"Jupiter =
(god)">Jupiter</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juno_(mytholog=
y)" title=3D"Juno (mythology)">Juno</a> (replacing <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Venus_(mythology)" title=3D"Venus (mythology)">Venus</a>),=
 and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturn_(mythology)" title=3D"=
Saturn (mythology)">Saturn</a>=E2=80=94arranged in an order encoding both t=
he Chaldean sequence of planets and the days of the <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Names_of_the_days_of_the_week" title=3D"Names of the days=
 of the week">Roman week</a>. The piece illustrates the <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syncretism" title=3D"Syncretism">syncretism</a> of Ca=
naanite, Greek, and Roman traditions, tracing the evolution of Heliopolitan=
 Jupiter from the Canaanite storm god <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Hadad#Hadad_in_Ugarit" title=3D"Hadad">Baal Hadad</a> into a cosmic dei=
ty of planetary order and prophecy. Named after its former owner, the Beiru=
ti aristocrat <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sursock_family" titl=
e=3D"Sursock family">Charles Sursock</a>, and acquired by the <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louvre" title=3D"Louvre">Louvre</a> in 1939, th=
e piece inaugurated the first issue of <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Syria_(journal)" title=3D"Syria (journal)">Syria</a></i>, the leadi=
ng French journal of Levantine archaeology, in 1920. <i>(<b><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sursock_bronze" title=3D"Sursock bronze">Full&#16=
0;article...</a></b>)</i>
</p>
