img alt=3D"Physella acuta" src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co=
mmons/thumb/5/54/Blasenschnecke_01.jpg/250px-Blasenschnecke_01.jpg" decodin=
g=3D"async" width=3D"162" height=3D"122" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-fil=
e-width=3D"3893" data-file-height=3D"2920"></a></span></div></div>
<p><i><b><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physella_acuta" title=3D"=
Physella acuta">Physella acuta</a></b></i> is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Species" title=3D"Species">species</a> of small, air-breathin=
g <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freshwater_snail" title=3D"Fresh=
water snail">freshwater snail</a> in the family <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Physidae" title=3D"Physidae">Physidae</a> of North American o=
rigin. Like other physids, <i>P.&#160;acuta</i> presents a sinistral (left-=
coiling) shell as well as a unique set of muscles called the physid muscula=
ture that allows it to rapidly twist the shell as a <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-predator_adaptation" title=3D"Anti-predator adaptati=
on">defence mechanism</a>. <i>Physella&#160;acuta</i> is <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invasive_species" title=3D"Invasive species">invasiv=
e</a> outside its native range. Its first introduction outside North Americ=
a likely occurred through the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hist=
ory_of_cotton" title=3D"History of cotton">18th-century cotton trade</a> to=
 Europe, with later spread mainly through the <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Fishkeeping" title=3D"Fishkeeping">aquarium trade</a>. The spec=
ies can occupy diverse freshwater habitats and tolerates polluted as well a=
s <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eutrophication" title=3D"Eutroph=
ication">oxygen-poor</a> environments. Due to its high reproductive rate an=
d tolerance to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitat_destruction=
" title=3D"Habitat destruction">habitat degradation</a>, it frequently <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition_(biology)" title=3D"Compet=
ition (biology)">outcompetes</a> native snail species. In aquariums, <i>P.&=
#160;acuta</i> is usually introduced through <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Ornamental_plant" title=3D"Ornamental plant">ornamental plants</=
a> and can become a "nuisance snail" due to its rapid reproduction. However=
, a controlled population can help clean up organic leftovers and control a=
lgae. <i>(<b><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physella_acuta" title=
=3D"Physella acuta">Full&#160;article...</a></b>)</i>
</p>
